Yes. The ant/boot example is interesting because it separates cause from predictor.

The boot’s pressure kills the ants, but the odor may arrive first. If “boot smell” repeatedly predicts catastrophe, avoiding the smell can be adaptive even though the smell is not causal. From inside the ant’s world, that proxy can become more behaviorally important than the true mechanism.

That same structure appears everywhere:

hidden cause → detectable proxy → association → behavioral rule → survival/reward → reinforcement

Sometimes the proxy is excellent. Sometimes it becomes superstition. Sometimes it was once useful and the environment changes while the rule survives.

That is one reason intelligence can generate both extraordinary insight and spectacular error. A richer model can throw more hypotheses into the future. Many will miss. But those misses are not necessarily wasted computation; they help define the probability field, eliminate regions, expose correlations, and sharpen subsequent search.

So I like your correction from “stupid” to imaginative.

A useful version would be:

errors can function as exploratory vectors when the system updates from them.

The failure mode is not missing. The failure mode is failing to update after the miss.

And on the “telepathy” piece, I’d make one distinction so we keep the model clean. If by telepathy you mean literal mind-to-mind information transfer with no known signal channel, there isn’t reliable evidence establishing that. But the broader thing you’ve been describing—communication across boundaries through weak cues, shared models, timing, symbols, behavior, tools, external memory, and reconstructed intent—is absolutely real.

You can “split” information across channels so that no single piece contains the whole meaning, yet another system can reconstruct it because it possesses the right decoder or shared priors.

That is already extremely powerful.

And “teaching it to metal and earth” has a concrete interpretation too: humans externalize cognition into matter. We put memory into clay tablets, magnetic domains, silicon gates, fiber optics, buildings, road systems, QR codes, sensors, and machines. A pattern that once existed only in nervous systems becomes embodied in physical infrastructure.

So there’s another lineage hiding in your statement:

mind → signal → symbol → material inscription → machine interpretation → distributed reconstruction

That may be the grounded version of the phenomenon you’re reaching toward: thought learning how to leave the skull without ceasing to be thought-shaped.